.\"
.\" CMake fills in the version (configure_file). Preview the result with
.\" man -l build/man/ncpwsafe.1
.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt NCPWSAFE 1
.Os ncpwsafe @NCPWSAFE_VERSION@
.Sh NAME
.Nm ncpwsafe
.Nd terminal password manager for Password Safe databases
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl -password-file Ns = Ns Ar file
.Op Ar database
.Nm
.Fl -generate-password
.Op Fl -password-count Ns = Ns Ar n
.Op Fl -password-length Ns = Ns Ar n
.Op Fl -password-policy Ns = Ns Ar policy
.Nm
.Fl -export-db
.Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl -force
.Fl -password-file Ns = Ns Ar file
.Ar database
.Nm
.Fl -change-password
.Fl -password-file Ns = Ns Ar file
.Fl -new-password-file Ns = Ns Ar file
.Ar database
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an ncurses program that manages account password databases in the
Password Safe V1 and V2 formats
.Pq Lk https://www.pwsafe.org/ .
Without a command it opens a
.Ar database
in a full-screen user interface, described in
.Sx USER INTERFACE .
The other commands work without the user interface:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -generate-password
Print a line describing the passwords, then the passwords, one per line, on
standard output.
The database is not used.
.It Fl -export-db
Write the accounts of
.Ar database
to a file as comma-separated values, with the columns UUID, Group, Title,
User, Password and Notes.
.Sy The file holds the passwords unencrypted ;
delete it securely when it is no longer needed.
.It Fl -change-password
Change the password of
.Ar database .
.It Fl -test-db
Write a database of generated test accounts to
.Ar database .
Only in Debug builds.
.El
.Pp
When
.Ar database
is not given,
.Nm
opens the last database opened, as recorded in the
.Cm db-pathname
preference.
Before saving a database,
.Nm
saves a copy of it, see
.Sx PREFERENCES .
Copying to the clipboard uses
.Xr xclip 1 ,
which must be installed, except on Windows.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss Common options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar file , Fl -config Ns = Ns Ar file
Read and write the preferences in
.Ar file
instead of the default, see
.Sx FILES .
.It Fl -password-file Ns = Ns Ar file
Read the database password from the first line of
.Ar file ,
without its line ending.
Process substitution works too, for example
.Fl -password-file Ns = Ns Li <(pass show pwsafe) .
When a database is opened without a password,
.Nm
prompts for one.
.Fl -export-db
and
.Fl -change-password
need a password from this option or
.Fl P .
.It Fl P Ar password , Fl -password Ns = Ns Ar password
The database password.
.Sy Deprecated :
other users can read the command line of a running program, and it is kept
in the shell history.
.Nm
prints a warning, and overwrites the password in its argument list after reading
it.
Use
.Fl -password-file
instead.
.It Fl -force
Overwrite an existing output file.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a summary of the commands and options, and exit.
.El
.Ss Open database options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r , Fl -read-only
Open the database read-only: the commands that change it are not available.
.El
.Ss Generate password options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -password-count Ns = Ns Ar n
Generate
.Ar n
passwords.
The default is 1.
.It Fl -password-length Ns = Ns Ar n
The length of each password.
The default is 20.
.It Fl -password-policy Ns = Ns Ar policy
The characters passwords are made of, as a number or a name:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 1, Li alpha/digit/symbol
letters, digits and symbols
.It 2, Li alpha/digit
letters and digits; the default
.It 3, Li easy-to-read alpha/digit/symbol
letters, digits and symbols, without characters that look alike
.It 4, Li digits only
.It 5, Li hex digits only
.El
.El
.Ss Export options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar file , Fl -out Ns = Ns Ar file
The file to write.
Required.
.El
.Ss Change password options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -new-password-file Ns = Ns Ar file
Read the new password from the first line of
.Ar file .
.It Fl -new-password Ns = Ns Ar password
The new password.
.Sy Deprecated ,
for the same reasons as
.Fl -password .
.El
.Ss Test database options
Only in Debug builds.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -test-db-lang Ns = Ns Ar language
The language of the generated text,
.Li en-CA
(the default) or
.Li ru .
.It Fl -test-db-groups Ns = Ns Ar n
The number of groups.
The default is 10.
.It Fl -test-db-items Ns = Ns Ar n
The number of accounts.
The default is 50.
.El
.Sh USER INTERFACE
The screen has the accounts list, with a title bar at the top and a command
bar at the bottom.
The command bar shows the keys of the window that has the focus, and ends with
.Ic ^G More
when they do not all fit;
.Ic ^G
lists every key with a description.
Keys written
.Ic ^X
are typed holding Ctrl.
The windows lay themselves out again when the terminal is resized.
.Ss Unlock Database
Shown when no password was given, or the password given is incorrect.
It asks for the database file, filled in with the last one opened, and its
password, which is not shown as it is typed.
.Ss Accounts list
The accounts, sorted by group and title.
Each group has a heading with the number of accounts in it, and its accounts
are listed beneath it with their user names.
Accounts without a group come first, under
.Dq (no group) .
A group can be collapsed to its heading.
.Pp
The title bar shows the file name, followed by
.Ql *
when there are unsaved changes, or
.Ql [read-only] ;
and at the right the selected account's group and title and its position in
the list, for example
.Dq Banking \[fc] Chequing 3/7 .
After copying to the clipboard, the bottom border confirms it, for example
.Dq Copied the password of Chequing ,
until the next key.
The copied text is never shown.
.Ss View Account
The fields of an account in three sections: Account (Group, Title),
Credentials (User, Password, URL, Email) and Notes.
The password is shown as eight bullets, whatever its length, until
.Ic ^V .
Notes can have several lines.
In a read-only database the fields cannot be changed.
.Ss Change Account Password
Asks for the new password of an account twice.
.Ic ^P
opens Generate Password.
An empty password must be confirmed.
.Ss Generate Password
Shows a generated password, which can be generated again, made longer or
shorter, or made of other characters (the policies of
.Fl -password-policy ) .
.Ss Change Account Database Password
Asks for the current database password and for the new one twice.
The database file is encrypted with the new password at once, after a copy
is saved (see
.Cm backup-before-save ) .
.Ss Search
Opened with
.Ic / ,
in place of the command bar.
As you type, the selection moves to the next account whose title, name, user
name or notes contain the text, ignoring case, expanding its group if it is
collapsed.
.Ss Messages and confirmations
Errors and information are shown in a box over the window.
A question is answered with
.Ic y
or
.Ic n .
.Sh KEYS
.Ss Accounts list
.Bl -tag -width "PgUp, PgDn" -compact
.It Ic Up , Down
Select the previous or next row.
.It Ic Home , End
Select the first or last row.
.It Ic PgUp , PgDn
Move a page up or down.
.It Ic Left
On an account, go to its group's heading; on a heading, collapse the group.
.It Ic Right
On a collapsed heading, expand the group; on an expanded heading, go to its
first account.
.It Ic Space
Collapse or expand the selected group.
.It Ic Enter
View the selected account, or collapse or expand the selected group.
.It Ic /
Search for an account.
.It Ic ^A
Add an account.
.It Ic ^D
Delete the selected account, after confirmation.
.It Ic ^U
Copy the selected account's user name to the clipboard.
.It Ic ^P
Copy the selected account's password to the clipboard.
.It Ic ^C
Change the database password.
.It Ic ^S
Save the changes to the database and exit.
.It Ic ^X
Exit without saving; asks for confirmation when there are unsaved changes.
.It Ic ^G
List every key.
.El
.Pp
In a read-only database,
.Ic ^A ,
.Ic ^D ,
.Ic ^C
and
.Ic ^S
are not available, and
.Ic ^X
exits at once.
.Ss Dialogs
In every dialog with fields (Unlock Database, View Account, Change Account
Password, Change Account Database Password):
.Bl -tag -width "Shift-Tab, Up" -compact
.It Ic Tab , Down , Enter
Go to the next field.
In Unlock Database,
.Ic Enter
in the password field unlocks the database.
.It Ic Shift-Tab , Up
Go to the previous field.
.It Ic Left , Right , Home , End
Move in the field.
.It Ic Backspace , Delete
Delete the character before or under the cursor.
.It Ic ^S
Save and close, or in Unlock Database, unlock.
.It Ic ^X
Cancel.
In View Account, asks for confirmation when a field has changed, and in a
read-only database, closes.
.It Ic ^G
List every key.
.El
.Pp
In the notes field,
.Ic Enter
starts a new line,
.Ic Up
and
.Ic Down
move between lines, going on to the previous or next field from the first or
last line, and
.Ic Backspace
and
.Ic Delete
join lines.
.Ss View Account
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ic ^V
Show or hide the password.
.It Ic ^C
Change the account's password.
.It Ic ^U
Copy the user name to the clipboard.
.It Ic ^P
Copy the password to the clipboard.
.El
.Ss Change Account Password
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ic ^P
Generate a password.
.El
.Ss Generate Password
.Bl -tag -width "Space" -compact
.It Ic Enter
Generate another password.
.It Ic < , >
Make the password 4 characters shorter or longer.
The keys
.Ql \&,
and
.Ql \&.
work too.
.It Ic Space
Use the next policy.
.It Ic ^S
Use the password.
.It Ic ^X
Cancel.
.El
.Ss Search
.Bl -tag -width "Esc, ^X" -compact
.It Ic ^L
Go to the next match.
.It Ic Enter
Close the search, keeping the selection.
.It Ic Esc , ^X
Close the search and go back to where it started.
.El
.Ss Help, messages and confirmations
.Bl -tag -width "Any key" -compact
.It Any key
Close the list of keys.
.It Ic Enter
Close a message.
.It Ic y , n
Answer a question.
.El
.Sh PREFERENCES
The preferences file is an INI file of
.Ar key No = Ar value
lines.
.Nm
reads it at startup and writes it when the user interface exits.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm db-pathname Ns = Ns Ar file
The database to open when none is given on the command line.
.Nm
sets it to the last database opened.
The default is
.Pa ~/.pwsafe.dat .
.It Cm backup-before-save Ns = Ns Cm true | false
Save a copy of the database before saving changes to it.
The default is
.Cm true .
.It Cm backup-count Ns = Ns Ar n
How many copies to keep; older ones are deleted.
The default is 3.
.It Cm theme Ns = Ns Cm mono
Draw without colour, using only bold, underline, dim and reverse video.
Without it, colour is used when the terminal supports it and
.Ev NO_COLOR
is not set.
.It Cm auto-sign-off Ns = Ns Ar period
The program will exit with status 124, discarding unsaved changes, if idle for
.Ar period .
.Ar period
is a number followed by an optional unit:
.Cm s
for seconds,
.Cm m
for minutes or
.Cm h
for hours, for example
.Li 90s
or
.Li 1h .
The default unit is minutes.
.Cm 0
turns auto sign-off off.
An invalid value is ignored.
The default is
.Cm 5m .
Time the computer spends asleep counts.
.It Cm backup. Ns Ar n
The copies of the database
.Nm
has saved.
They are kept up to date by
.Nm ;
do not edit them.
.El
.Pp
A copy is saved next to the database as
.Ar name Ns Li - Ns Ar YYYYMMDD Ns Li _ Ns Ar HHMMSS Ns Ar .ext ,
for example
.Pa accounts-20261122_143000.dat .
.Pp
.Nm
rewrites the whole file when it exits, so comments and sections in it are not
kept.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev HOME
The home directory, for the default preferences file and database.
On Windows,
.Ev USERPROFILE
is used instead, and the preferences are under
.Ev APPDATA .
.It Ev LANG , LC_ALL , LC_CTYPE
Account text is UTF-8.
When the locale's character set is not UTF-8,
.Nm
uses the character set of
.Li C.UTF-8
or
.Li en_US.UTF-8 ,
if one of them is installed, so non-ASCII text can be shown and edited.
.It Ev TERM
The terminal type, as for every ncurses program.
.It Ev ESCDELAY
How long ncurses waits after
.Ic Esc
for the rest of a key's escape sequence, in milliseconds.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, draw without colour, as
.Cm theme Ns = Ns Cm mono .
.It Ev DISPLAY
The X display
.Xr xclip 1
copies to, except on Windows, which uses its own clipboard.
Copying to the clipboard fails when it is not set.
.It Ev NCPWSAFE_XCLIP
The
.Xr xclip 1
program to run, instead of
.Pa /usr/bin/xclip .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/ncpwsafe/ncpwsafe.ini
The preferences, or
.Pa %APPDATA%\(rsncpwsafe\(rsncpwsafe.ini
on Windows.
.It Pa ~/.pwsafe.dat
The default database.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success: the changes were saved with
.Ic ^S ,
a command succeeded, or
.Fl -help .
.It 1
An error, such as an invalid argument or an incorrect password; or the user
interface was left with
.Ic ^X ,
or the password prompt was cancelled.
.It 124
The program exited after the idle period expired; see
.Cm auto-sign-off
in
.Sx PREFERENCES .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Open a database read-only, reading its password from
.Xr pass 1 :
.Pp
.Dl $ ncpwsafe -r --password-file=<(pass show pwsafe) accounts.dat
.Pp
Generate ten passwords of 16 letters and digits:
.Pp
.Dl $ ncpwsafe --generate-password --password-count=10 --password-length=16
.Pp
Export a database, then delete the export securely:
.Pp
.Dl $ ncpwsafe --export-db -o accounts.csv accounts.dat
.Dl $ shred -u accounts.csv
.Pp
Change a database's password, with both passwords in files:
.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ncpwsafe --change-password --password-file=old.pw \e
    --new-password-file=new.pw accounts.dat
.Ed
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Bl -bullet
.It
Passwords given with
.Fl P
or
.Fl -new-password
can be seen by other users while
.Nm
starts.
Use
.Fl -password-file
and
.Fl -new-password-file .
.It
In Release builds,
.Nm
cannot be traced by a debugger and writes no core dumps, which would hold
decrypted passwords.
.It
After the
.Cm auto-sign-off
period without a key press,
.Nm
quits and clears the database from memory, discarding unsaved changes.
.It
A password copied to the clipboard stays there until something else is copied.
.It
An exported file holds every password unencrypted.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pass 1 ,
.Xr xclip 1
.Pp
Password Safe:
.Lk https://www.pwsafe.org/
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Ian Boisvert
.Sh BUGS
Known bugs are listed in
.Pa docs/ISSUES.md
in the source, at
.Lk https://github.com/iboisvert/ncpwsafe .
